  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>At a 5% duty assumption, a €10,000 CIF shipment clears at €12,705: €500 duty (5% × 10,000), then 21% VAT × €10,500 = €2,205, a 27.05% uplift (1.05 × 1.21 = 1.2705).</li>
      <li>The 21% VAT on CIF + duty equals 22.05% of CIF (21% × 1.05), so it adds €105 more than flat 21% on €10,000 CIF (2,205 − 2,100).</li>
      <li>Article 23 defers the full €2,205 VAT on a €10,000 CIF shipment but the 5% duty of €500 is still payable at import; the benefit is cash timing, not a €2,205 cost saving.</li>
      <li>The page's illustrative midpoint gives a 27.3 CBM FCL/LCL crossover (US$3,000 ÷ US$110 = 27.3), while 15 CBM at US$110/CBM costs US$1,650 — US$1,350 below the 20ft midpoint; the actual split swings widely with the US$60–160/CBM LCL range.</li>
      <li>Rail at 18–22 days saves 8–12 days versus the 30-day sea window — 27–40% of the sea time (8 ÷ 30 = 27%; 12 ÷ 30 = 40%) — for roughly 2–3× the sea rate, so the premium must be checked against carrying cost, not just calendar days.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

